course: dictionary
lesson: 1974
title: Completion: when the job is done
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Completion means finishing something, so it is all done. «On completion of the task» means when you have finished the task.
explain: Completion can also mean the state of being complete, or whole. Look at the root word: complete. It ends in te, so it takes -tion to make the noun: complete, completion.
explain: Context helps you work out the meaning. In building and projects, «completion date» is the day something will be finished. In maths, «percentage completion» shows how much of a job is done.
fact: Completion means finishing something, so it is all done.
fact: Roots ending in t or te take -tion: complete, completion.
fact: «Near completion» means almost finished.
fact: «Percentage completion» shows how much of a job is done, such as 50 percent completion means half is done.
fact: Completion is usually uncountable, so we talk about completion, not many completions.
exercise: «On completion of the task, hand in your sheet.» What does on completion mean here? | While you are talking about the task | *When the task is finished | Instead of the task | Before you start the task
exercise: A progress bar shows 75% completion. What does this show? | *Three-quarters of the job is done | The job is only a quarter done | The job has not started | It is all finished, 100 percent
exercise: Which noun is made from the root complete? | completing | complete | *completion | completely
test: «The completion date for the bridge is next spring.» What is the completion date? | The day the bridge was painted red | The day the bridge is closed for repairs | *The day the bridge will be finished | The day the bridge was designed
test: «Her homework is near completion.» What does near completion mean? | Just starting | Lost under the bed | Very big and heavy | *Almost finished
exam: Invent becomes invention, and complete becomes completion. Which noun is made from the root invent in the same way? | inventing | inventor | invented | *invention
